Month = {APR 30},
Abstract = {In deserts, water has been singled out as the most important factor for
   choosing where to settle, but trees were likely an important part of the
   landscape for hunter - gatherers beyond merely constituting an economic
   resource. Yet, this critical aspect has not been considered
   archaeologically. Here, we present the results of mapping and
   radiocarbon dating of a truly unique archaeological record. Over 150
   preserved stumps around five Late Pleistocene/ Early Holocene
   archaeological campsites (12,800 to 11,200 cal BP) show that trees were
   key features in the creation of everyday habitats for the first
   inhabitants of the Atacama Desert. At two of these sites, QM12 and QM35,
   the spatial and chronological correlation between trees and hearths
   reveals that people located their homes under the tree canopy. At
   residential site QM35, artifact distribution coincides with a grove
   dated to - 11,600 to 11,200 cal BP. A third residential area (QM32)
   occurred along the grove margins - 12,000 to 11,200 cal BP. Based on the
   distinct cultural material of these two camps, we propose that two
   different groups intermittently shared this rich wetland - grove
   environment. The tree taxa suggest a preference for the native Schinus
   molle, a tree scarcely present on the landscape today, over the endemic,
   nitrogen - fixing Strombocarpa tamarugo , both for toolmaking and
   firewood and even though the S. tamarugo was locally more abundant.
   Together with the spatial and chronological coincidence of campsites,
   hearths, and trees, we propose that people spared the most abundant and
   resilient species to create their homes, in turn promoting fertility
   oases amid the Atacama's hyperaridity.},
