Title = {Competitive inhibition and mutualistic growth in co-infections:
   deciphering Staphylococcus aureus-Acinetobacter baumannii interaction
   dynamics},
Journal = {ISME COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2024},
Volume = {4},
Number = {1},
Month = {JUL 3},
Abstract = {Staphylococcus aureus (Sa) and Acinetobacter baumannii (Ab) are
   frequently co-isolated from polymicrobial infections that are severe and
   refractory to therapy. Here, we apply a combination of wet-lab
   experiments and in silico modeling to unveil the intricate nature of the
   Ab/Sa interaction using both, representative laboratory strains and
   strains co-isolated from clinical samples. This comprehensive
   methodology allowed uncovering Sa's capability to exert a partial
   interference on Ab by the expression of phenol-soluble modulins. In
   addition, we observed a cross-feeding mechanism by which Sa supports the
   growth of Ab by providing acetoin as an alternative carbon source. This
   study is the first to dissect the Ab/Sa interaction dynamics wherein
   competitive and cooperative strategies can intertwine. Through our
   findings, we illuminate the ecological mechanisms supporting their
   coexistence in the context of polymicrobial infections. Our research not
   only enriches our understanding but also opens doors to potential
   therapeutic avenues in managing these challenging infections.
   Graphical AbstractSummary of the Ab/Sa interaction during co-infections.
   Contact-independent mechanisms such as the secretion of quorum sensing
   (QS) molecules like phenol-soluble molecule alpha (PSM alpha) by Sa or
   N-homoserine lactones by Ab drive competitive interactions. In addition,
   Ab can compete with Sa via the expression of the type VI secretion
   system (T6SS) and can affect Sa in a contact-dependent manner. In
   contrast, both species cooperate by the cross-feeding of the alternative
   carbon source acetoin, which is produced by Sa and consumed by Ab.},
